!! Red-first test design — the procedure, not the slogan

"Vector first, fix second" names a discipline that fails silently when run loosely. Run it as eight
ordered moves; a skipped move turns the whole thing into decoration.

# ''Write the vector before the cure.'' The vector names the hole. A vector written after the cure tests the cure's shape, never the hole's.
# ''Watch it fail, and READ the failure text.'' A vector that passes on its first run measured nothing — either the hole never existed, or the vector misses it. Both readings demand work before any cure gets written.
# ''Check the red fails for the stated reason.'' Read the message, never the colour. A vector that reds on a typo, a missing fixture, or an unrelated throw looks identical to a vector that reds on the defect.
# ''Run the nonsense control.'' Feed the gate something that MUST fail — a nonsense verb, a mangled digest, an address nothing holds. A control that passes proves the gate reads nothing, and a green from that gate carries no information.
# ''Assert the setup took.'' A fixture that mutates a live carrier MUST assert its own mutation landed (`.not.toBe(source)`). A silent no-op replace leaves disk == synced == canonical, and four vectors then agree with each other about nothing.
# ''Match at a dispatch position.'' Assert against the value the code branches on, never against a string that merely appears nearby. A mention satisfies a grep; only a dispatch satisfies a caller.
# ''Cure, re-run, and check BOTH marks move.'' The vector goes green AND the nonsense control stays red. One without the other names a cure that widened the gate instead of closing the hole.
# ''A red that clears with no edit names shared state, never a cure.'' `dist/` and `bags/` carry other writers, usually the operator. `git status` before believing any red, and before believing any green that follows one.

''Where a red belongs when nothing can cure it yet.'' A law the models imply and no code enforces gets
DECLARED in the red register (`packages/lararium-mesh/tests/unbuilt-laws.test.ts`), skipped, one law per
entry. `tools/unbuilt-laws-witness.sh` strips the skips and reports any red that GREENED — which catches
the case where the system starts keeping a law by accident and nobody notices. A declared red counts as
work landed, never as work deferred.

!! Wiring a module that stands designed, tested, and unreached

Three entries sit in the census above. Each passes its own tests and no production path calls it. The
temptation reads as "it works, just call it" — and that skips the move that matters. Walk the ladder:

# ''Census before anything.'' Grep counts MENTIONS. Follow the call. A hit inside a comment saying "this gate cannot reach it" reads to `grep` exactly like a caller.
# ''Name what the module needs that nothing supplies.'' Every unreached module sits unreached for a reason, and the reason lives UPSTREAM of the call site. `vesselDyads` wants a veil; nothing mints one. Write that missing input down as a Stage 0 question before touching a call site.
# ''If Stage 0 resolves to an operator ruling, STOP and say so.'' Do NOT default the absence. Defaulting an absence reads as correct WITHIN an axis and as a fabricated verdict ACROSS one — and a wiring choice made to unblock a task quietly rules the design question the operator held open.
# ''Red-first at the CALL SITE, never in the module's own test.'' The module's tests already pass; that fact defines the problem. The new vector asserts that the production path produces the module's effect — and it MUST red before the wire lands, for the reason "nothing calls it".
# ''Wire the read path before the write path.'' A read-path wire gets observed without changing state, so a wrong reading surfaces as a wrong answer rather than as corrupted durable data. Land it, watch it in a real run, then wire the write.
# ''Land the module behind its existing signature.'' Widening a type to fit the first caller lets the caller's shape leak into the model. If the caller cannot supply what the signature asks, return to move 2 — the missing input never went away, it just moved.
# ''Re-run the suite AND the built binary.'' Typecheck reads `src/`; the binary reads `dist/`. A cross-package wire passes typecheck and dies at runtime whenever `dist/` lags.
# ''Retire the "specified" label in the SAME commit that wires it.'' Move the entry out of the census here. A census that keeps naming something already wired teaches the next spirit to distrust the census.

''Naming law.'' Until a production path calls it, the module stands ''specified in code'' — never "built",
never "done", never "ready". The three words differ, and only the first one holds true.

!! Instruments that read green while reporting something false

Carried forward from the grammar watch and extended by this one. Collide each before trusting it:

* ''A digest gate'' answers "do these bytes match this hash", never "do these bytes belong here".
* ''A fixture pins the grammar; only the corpus pins the reader.'' A hand-written fixture agreed with itself and disagreed with 639 files out of 639.
* ''A grep for callers'' counts mentions, including comments that say the caller does not exist. Follow the call; never grep the door.
* ''A blanket re-stamp signs edits nobody authored.'' `lares meme normalize` names its files and takes no glob, because a re-stamp driven by `git diff` will sign the OTHER writer's work — and it NAMES every dirty carrier the run passed over, since a carrier nobody named reads two ways and the door cannot tell which.
* ''The other writer usually turns out to be the operator.'' Parallel `lares` sessions share this tree. `git status` before believing any red; a dirty file of unknown provenance gets REPORTED, copied aside, never reverted.

!! Parallel watches standing right now

Two other `lares` sessions work this tree. Their closing bearings, so a fresh spirit does not re-open their ground:

* ''Grammar extension'' (`@hearth`) — `enumeration.derived.debt-named`, and before it `every.value.reads-as-what-renders-it` · `twenty-six.ruled.forty-three.remain`. The grammar submodule stands witnessed.
* ''Corpus / panel'' (`@crossroads`) — `panel.carried.to-the-seed/prose-still-lags`. The confidence dial left the repo and reached the boot seed; the prose describing it lags the shape.

