<<~ ahu #/shift-grammar-watch>>

! SHIFT CHANGE — the grammar watch (handed 2026-08-29)

The git log holds what landed. This names the ground, the paths open from it, and the instruments
that read GREEN while reporting something false — each one measured, each one cheap to repeat.

!! The ground, measured at handoff

| ''front'' | ''reading'' |
|extension `VSCode-TW5-Syntax` |local 2.3.0 unreleased · marketplace serves 2.2.1 · 4,191 installs |
|gates |7 green · corpus 31 files, 433 scopes declared, 332 reached (floor 332) · containment 0 of 31 |
|dark verdicts |''none'' — every `invalid.*` the grammar declares stands exercised by a corpus specimen |
|tree-sitter |43 named node kinds · ''0'' lexicon captures in `queries/highlights.scm` · 3 parity specimens · 3 gates green |
|house |vessel stands · `bcc-witness` 614 ok / 0 mismatch · submission shelf 0 of 5 out of step |
|`recall` |''DOWN'' — answers `content holder exited (code null)` while every mempalace check reads ok. Undiagnosed |
|upstream |PR 9920 OPEN mergeable, unreviewed since 2026-08-27 · PR 9921 DRAFT, untouched since 2026-07-12 · PR 9999 OPEN · issue 9997 OPEN, unlabelled |

!!! The host corpus — rebuild it, never look for it

Measuring the grammar against TiddlyWiki's own wikis needs a corpus, and the one used here lived in a
scratchpad that does not survive a session. Rebuilding costs one command each:

```
# unbiased: every 14th tiddler across editions, core, plugins, themes
find TiddlyWiki5/{editions,core,plugins,themes} -name '*.tid' | sort | awk 'NR % 14 == 0' | head -400 > wide.txt
i=0; while read f; do i=$((i+1)); awk 'BEGIN{b=0} /^$/{if(!b){b=1;next}} b{print}' "$f" > "D/w$(printf '%03d' $i).tw"; done < wide.txt
paste -d'\t' <(seq -f 'w%03g' 1 400) wide.txt > manifest.tsv   # maps specimen back to source file
```

Then `source ./grammars.sh` and `npx vscode-tmgrammar-snap "${ARGS[@]}" -s text.html.tiddlywiki5 -u D/*.tw`,
and tally with `grep -o 'invalid[a-z.-]*' D/*.snap | sort | uniq -c`. ''The manifest matters'': a hit
means little until it names the file it came from.

!! The paths, and what each costs

!!! 1 — The verdict tail, and the instrument it needs first

Eleven `bad-angle-bracket` hits stand across 400 unbiased host tiddlers, and they read as ''three
families, never one'':

* ''a filter variable reference'' — `[tag<tag>!has]` in `core/wiki/macros/keyboard-driven-input.tid`, 4 hits. The bracket opening `<var>` inside a filter run.
* ''a triple-quoted attribute string'' — `["<-"] {x} ["->"]` in the fr-FR railroad plugin, 2 hits. Literal angle brackets inside `"""…"""`.
* ''TiddlyWiki's own malformed fixtures'' — `editions/test/tiddlers/tests/data/parse-backcompat/`, 2 hits. Deliberate garbage, so those verdicts likely EARN their place. A cure that silences all three silences a true reading.

''A slicer comes before any fix here.'' A specimen rebuilt by hand reproduces the host's reading
poorly — measured at four consecutive failures — because the surrounding file supplies the parse state
and a hand-made shape strips it away. The tool owed: map a snapshot hit to its file and line through
the manifest, then slice the REAL bytes around it and assert on those.

!!! 2 — Publish, or rule against it

A body of measured fixes stands unshipped against 4,191 installs. ''Versions defer by operator
ruling'': ONE unreleased version carries the accumulated work, and a bump waits for a release rather
than riding each commit.

!!! 3 — The weld

tree-sitter models memetic-wikitext's BLOCK STRUCTURE — 43 kinds: fences, quotes, pragmas, tables,
typed and style blocks, hard breaks. The TextMate grammar models its LEXICON — carriers, `lar://`
addresses, blockcheck, sharkteeth, bearings, anchors, `key=value`. They overlap at the sigil family
alone, and `queries/highlights.scm` carries ZERO captures for the spec's own constructs. Its gates
read green over three specimens, so green there names very little.

The cheaper move first: a weld GATE failing when a scope family has no node kind turns the gap into a
number that cannot drift back. Teaching tree-sitter the lexicon stands behind it.

!!! 4 — PR 9920, where a person waits

A maintainer asked on 2026-08-27 whether the parser diagnostics justify the code they add. The reply
promised perf numbers. The harness for exactly that measurement sits built in draft PR 9921 —
`perf-replay-command`, `perf-compare-command`, `stats`, with its own tests — and has never run. The
only cost figure standing names +16.2 KB on `empty.html`, with no parse-time beside it. A person waits,
and no technical blocker stands.

!!! 5 — The seam gate, generalized

`tests/seed-definition-matches-example.test.ts` welds ONE procedure — `~lares` — to the worked examples
printed beside it in the boot seed, because that pair contradicted itself for months while every gate
read green. The seed defines ''fifteen'' procedures. Nothing says the other fourteen agree, and
nothing says they disagree.

!!! 6 — Twenty-three embedded-language scopes stand dark

Fenced-code branches no corpus file exercises. Fixture debt falls due before a coverage number there
carries meaning.

!! Instruments that read GREEN while reporting something false

# ''A prose `#` line in a spec file reaches the grammar as WIKITEXT.'' `vscode-tmgrammar-test` treats only `#` lines carrying `^` or `<-` as assertions; every other one parses as source. A comment containing `@@` opens a style block and swallows the construct asserted below it, and the negative assertion then PASSES because the scope genuinely vanished. Spec prose admits no `@@`, no leading `!`, no pipe, no backtick, no `<tag`, no `{{` and no `[[`. Audit: `grep '^#' <spec> | grep -v '\^' | grep -E '@@|\||`|<[a-z$]'`.
# ''A negative assertion passes VACUOUSLY'' where the scope name or the column misses — measured three times over one watch, each looking like a clean pass. ''Make every negative go RED once on purpose.'' Calibrate a column by asserting PRESENCE at several indices; the snapshot format's caret convention differs from the test format's by one, so reading a column off a `.snap` misses.
# ''A grep over sigils written with `[^>]` stops at the bearing arrow.'' It produced three false surveys: one reporting a single opener shape where four stood, one hiding `family:` and `role:` entirely, one reporting 639 bare openers that did not exist. Use `(?:[^>\n]|>(?!>))`, and fence the boundary the claim names — a space before `?`, never a bare `?`.
# ''A corpus file ending on an unterminated construct swallows everything appended after it.'' Eleven specimens appended below a bare `{{` registered ZERO new scopes while firing correctly in a fresh file. The containment gate catches the reverse leak; suspicion catches this one.
# ''`snapshot-check`'s drift output truncates both sides at one width'', so a diff of its `pinned`/`now` lines can compare identical prefixes and report a vacuous match. Verify a re-pin with `git diff -U0` over the `.snap` files.
# ''A `git ls-files '*.mem'` sweep reaches frozen fixtures.'' `packages/tree-sitter-memetic-wikitext/fixtures/specimens/` hold pinned parity inputs and `packages/lararium-sensorium/scripts/fixtures/` belong to the kumulipo campaign. A sweep breaks both gates. Their standing in an OLDER carrier form marks them as frozen, never as stragglers.

!! The bearing this watch recommends

''PR 9920 (4) before the verdict tail (1).'' A maintainer's question already asked outweighs another
verdict retired, and the harness answering it already stands built. The tail teaches the instrument
the rest of the grammar work needs, so it follows well.

!! The law the grammar work stands on

''A verdict may stand only where TiddlyWiki REFUSES.'' Its parser raises no error — what it cannot read
falls through as text — so the only honest test reads what it BUILDS:

* the parser returns a ''node'' -> the construct works -> ''no verdict may stand'' (an INVENTION)
* the parser yields ''plain text'' -> the author reached for markup and missed -> the verdict earns its place

Ten inventions fell to that reading. Each surfaced by asking the host — its regexes, its parser, its
own shipped wikis — and none by reasoning about what the grammar ought to say.

