!! Burr ledger — live only; each carries its wake condition

Closed and folded into history (full narration in `git log` on this file, pre-2026-09-05): the ingest
fixture-drift reds; the realm idle clock (closed 2026-09-12, `BagStowage` cools by the realm's own rolls);
the two-sided relation reading (landed 2026-08-31, walked in docker); restart-blindness read as correct
and pinned; the mesh grid at 18/18 cells green with the cold-found patience fixed; the antigen share's
mandatory-tier ruling (it already stood ruled; stop re-opening it); the stale `cabal-realm.ts` comment,
corrected in source; the transfer-source gate (closed 2026-08-31, `crossingDirection` grades by direction);
the e2e harness drift measured 2026-09-01 (the counts in `er-handoff#/vitals` now supersede it); the two
instrument lies (the coverage-vs-pass distinction and the dist-reads-stale-build bug), generalized into the
standing law that each dist-reading witness refuses a stale build by name (`er-handoff#/cold-start`); the
nine-then-ten-scenario mesh-isolation note, superseded by the mesh grid above; the island→admin verb-plane
switchboard retirement and its `#ingest-ordering` hoike, superseded by the verb-pair and in-VM parse work
this house since built (`project_in_vm_parse_move.md`, `project_boot_holds_family_rewrite.md`).

Still open, wake condition carried forward:
* ''The cold-boot `unavailable` false-negative'' (automerge-repo). Root cause confirmed from upstream source: on a cold `create()` with no peers, storage answers `unavailable` immediately, by design. `allowableStates` reads deprecated upstream; callers moved to `findWithProgress`. One real ordering defect fixed (`init.ts` now flushes before it publishes the social-bootstrap pointer). An opt-in 2s grace landed for the two `@daemon` reads that expect presence — not a proven cure (six dirty/clean samples split roughly even under identical code). Wakes if the grace proves insufficient: read the per-source map (`peek().sources`) before widening any number again.
* ''One carrier fails the corpus round-trip'': `bags/elyncia/…/primary-characters/lady-aki.mem` does not render back; proven pre-existing and not a build gap. Wants an owner.
* ''Operator-ness sits outside the cap stack — enforced by disk access, never a capability.'' A browser vessel reaches no nexus/seal surface, so an operator's own browser device cannot exercise operator power. Wakes when operator power needs to reach a browser (an operator-capped leaf), which is the moment the seam must become a cap rather than a path.
* ''Co-hosted sovereignty stands claimed and measured, unenforced between same-uid peers.'' `identityHomeClosure` reports an open seal-home mode and never repairs it; a same-uid neighbour still reaches a peer vessel's identity dir whatever the bits hold, and the charter and persona-root seeds still write with no mode floor. Cure shape stands researched (an unforgeable handle, never a path string — Capsicum/`pledge`/Landlock; a delegation token across machines); nobody has built it.
* ''A deny-only antigen share is not the free lunch it reads as.'' Field evidence on fediverse blocklists (binary, grow-only, effectively permanent entries that sever third parties who never joined the threat model) argues for entries that expire, carried provenance, and a defined merge semantics before two antigen lists ever need merging. The mandatory-tier ruling stands; this is a caution on HOW the shared list behaves, not a reversal.
* ''One charter per vessel is a guard on a single-slot file, never a law.'' `larSealHome()` names one unparameterized seal home; multi-Nexus already stands canon and tested (`waking-floor:165`, `fleetSpan`) once a second seal home exists. R-CHARTER (`stable-founding-road#/the-road`) re-keys the chairs and re-scopes the keyring per relation; it does not yet give a vessel a second seal home. Wakes on that build.
* ''The crossing rail inherits three unbuilt requirements'': both ends of a crossing must arrive as capabilities the caller presents, never a cap plus a bare string naming the other side; the batch needs a saga shape (every validation before the first outward grant, idempotent and forward-retried after); and the union catalog must declare its deletion semantics before a deaccession can propagate. Research done, nothing built.
* ''The ledger-move collision against canon'' (a realm's maintenance tally, pre-authorised to move onto the shared substrate) carries three unpriced constraints: the slot-map shape assumes a fixed writer-set, which a realm's arriving dwellers violate; the residency cross-wire ban gets easy to trip once slots live in the substrate doc; and `forward_secrecy=false` lets a newly admitted dweller sync the entire historical tally. Fork the primitive rather than re-home it (`lamplighters:160`); the plumbing to address a realm substrate does not exist yet (`ResolveDaemonStore` takes no realm argument).
* ''Held, not acted on'' (operator, 2026-09-02): a Lares startup module inside the VM wiring whatever the projection environment lacks. The seam already exists and ships twice (`capture-annotate-vm.ts`, `grammar-cache.ts`). Read `project_off_main_thread_engine_laws.md` before firing it — that research has since landed and may answer this directly.
* Smaller carried threads, each with its own wake condition where the name does not already say it: the tombstone/whiteout verb (reserved, §3); Automerge history growth (DXOS-epoch pattern watched); `lares wiki` subcommands print human-only; an engine-swap reconcile that answered `current` against a stale engine; the NFC assertion at the ingest shore; the parser's retired typed-edge emission plane (`edge.ts`, `kumu-device.ts`); the module-gate positive-path vector (`bootTrustedModules`); a `TimeoutNegativeWarning` in e2e runs, pre-existing and unlocalized.

